240
00:24:52,000 --> 00:25:02,260
By morning, the Zulus are gone, and the post is still standing. Its new walls were made of grain sacks and biscuit boxes. Let's look at the ground and find out how they held.

241
00:25:02,780 --> 00:25:12,580
The post is called Rourke's Drift, a supply depot and field hospital for Lord Chelmsford's army. Around noon, the station commander rides off to hurry up reinforcements.

242
00:25:12,580 --> 00:25:24,120
He leaves a lieutenant of the Royal Engineers in charge. His name is John Chard. He has never been in a battle. With him is Lieutenant Gonville Bromhead and B Company of the 24th Foot.

243
00:25:25,000 --> 00:25:35,490
In the middle of the afternoon, two riders come in from the east. The main camp at Izandwana, about 10 km away, has been overrun. A Zulu force is on

244
00:25:35,490 --> 00:25:46,860
its way. Chard has no orders for this. He has about 110 fit British soldiers, a company of local African troops, more than 30 patients, two oxwagons, and very little time.

245
00:25:46,860 --> 00:25:59,894
This is the real ground with the heights stretched so you can read them The post sits on a gentle shelf above the Buffalo

246
00:25:59,894 --> 00:26:12,928
River Two buildings 35 yards apart In the west the hospital A long house of soft mud brick with a thatched roof and 11

247
00:26:12,928 --> 00:26:25,962
small rooms In the east the storehouse Until the army came it was the missionary church Behind them to the south rises a hill

248
00:26:25,962 --> 00:26:38,996
the British call the Oscarberg The Zulus call it Xi Its lower slope is full of rocks and caves H subscribe cho k G

249
00:26:39,016 --> 00:26:51,706
Play it out. Ox wagons move at walking pace. The patients cannot march. The Zulu regiments coming toward them have marched about 30 kilometers since 8 that morning. A man on foot is still faster than

250
00:26:51,706 --> 00:27:04,396
an ox. Somewhere on that road, in open grass, 4,000 men catch 150. That had already happened once that day. At Isandlwana, the British had no wall, and most of the camp died in the open.

251
00:27:04,396 --> 00:27:12,216
The acting commissary, James Dalton, says it out loud. Private Henry Hook gave his words years later.

252
00:27:12,696 --> 00:27:21,036
If we left the drift, every man was certain to be killed. So they stay. Staying is only half a plan. The other half is how much ground to hold.

253
00:27:21,036 --> 00:27:31,296
The British Army's field handbook of the time is Garnet Wolseley's Soldier's Pocketbook. The 1874 edition, under defensive posts, gives the arithmetic.

254
00:27:31,296 --> 00:27:35,636
YauTube, it assumes the garrison is sufficient for the extent to be defended.

255
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:45,660
In other words, about one file to every running yard of hedge, wall, and so on. A file is two men. Then a quarter more, held back as

256
00:27:45,660 --> 00:27:55,320
a reserve. The same page asks for a central building as a citadel, where all the wounded are carried, and it says this. Boxes, barrels, and bags

257
00:27:55,320 --> 00:28:04,980
filled with earth make good parapets. Now, count. About 110 fit men is 55 files. Keep a quarter back, and you can man under 50 yards of wall.

258
00:28:04,980 --> 00:28:14,667
The two buildings alone stand 35 yards apart. A wall around both of them and the kraal run several times longer than that. So the plan has two parts.

259
00:28:14,667 --> 00:28:24,353
Build the outer wall now as long as it has to be. And before the enemy arrives, build a second, shorter wall inside it so the line can shrink

260
00:28:24,353 --> 00:28:34,040
when the men run short. Hold only the wall you can fill and have the next one ready behind it. Mark that sentence. The whole night turns on it.

261
00:28:34,040 --> 00:28:45,216
The storehouse holds bags of maize called mealy and boxes of hard biscuit Hook remembered the mealy bags as good big heavy things weighing about 200 pounds each In about an hour and a half

262
00:28:45,216 --> 00:28:56,393
the men drag them into a wall about four feet high It runs from the far corner of the hospital along the rocky ledge on the north side to the stone crawl On the south

263
00:28:56,393 --> 00:29:07,570
it closes the gap back to the store The men knock loopholes through both buildings Then Chard adds the second wall a line of biscuit boxes straight across the middle of the yard from the storehouse

264
00:29:07,570 --> 00:29:18,746
to the north wall If the hospital end falls the men can step back behind it At about 4 there is firing behind the hill A troop of African horsemen posted out there rides away

265
00:29:18,746 --> 00:29:29,676
Then the company of the natal native contingent climbs over the wall and runs, its captain with it. In a few minutes, the garrison drops from about 400 to about 150. The

266
00:29:29,676 --> 00:29:40,606
wall is just as long, the men are not. At half past four, the first Zulus come around the shoulder of Xi'an, about 600 of them running straight at the south wall.

267
00:29:40,606 --> 00:29:45,666
Chard wrote that they rushed on in spite of their heavy loss to within 50 yards of the wall.

268
00:29:46,086 --> 00:29:50,206
Then they swing around the hospital, into the bush on the north side.

269
00:29:50,606 --> 00:29:54,526
Riflemen climb into the caves of Xi'an and fire down into the yard.

270
00:29:55,046 --> 00:29:58,166
From here on, every wall is under attack at once.

271
00:29:58,666 --> 00:30:00,386
The north wall takes the worst of it.

272
00:30:00,806 --> 00:30:03,246
Spear against bayonet across the mealy bags.

273
00:30:03,366 --> 00:30:07,246
The men there are spread thin, as the arithmetic said.

274
00:30:07,746 --> 00:30:09,886
Around 6, Chard reads it the same way.

275
00:30:09,886 --> 00:30:12,746
He wrote later that our line of defense was too extended.

276
00:30:13,286 --> 00:30:15,426
He pulls everyone back behind the biscuit boxes.

277
00:30:15,906 --> 00:30:17,326
The second wall is already there.

278
00:30:17,500 --> 00:30:25,360
This is the rule doing its work. The men stand closer together, and the open yard becomes ground the Zulus must cross under fire.

279
00:30:25,820 --> 00:30:32,640
But the hospital is now outside the line. Patients are still inside. The Zulus set fire to the thatch.

280
00:30:33,160 --> 00:30:37,820
Hook said the rooms were so small that you could hardly swing a bayonet in them.

281
00:30:38,240 --> 00:30:45,400
Hook and Private John Williams hack through the mudbrick partitions, dragging the patients room to room, then out a window and across the yard.

282
00:30:45,400 --> 00:30:55,247
The breaching takes about 50 minutes. Of 11 patients, 9 reach the line alive. Around 10, the men give up the outer half of the kraal. The line shrinks again.

283
00:30:55,247 --> 00:31:05,093
In the yard, two big heaps of mealy bags become a hollow redoubt about 9 feet high. Chard wrote that it gave a second line of fire all around.

284
00:31:05,093 --> 00:31:14,940
Wolseley's Citadel, made of grain sacks. The burning hospital lights the yard for the riflemen. The attacks slow after midnight. The firing ends a little after 4 in the morning.

285
00:31:15,400 --> 00:31:25,968
Now the count The closest study of the roll puts the garrison at 154 men 17 are killed or die of wounds 15 are wounded Of about 20

286
00:31:25,968 --> 00:31:36,535
rounds in reserve roughly 900 are left Chard called it about a box and a half The Zulu count is harder The next day the British buried 351

287
00:31:36,535 --> 00:31:47,103
bodies near the post Other estimates run to 500 600 and higher More bodies were found later and many wounded Zulus were killed after the fight The

288
00:31:47,103 --> 00:31:57,671
counts differ and that last fact belongs in the record too 11 Victoria Crosses were awarded still the most for a single action So why did it hold

289
00:31:57,671 --> 00:32:01,951
The Zulus were brave, and they outnumbered the garrison more than 20 to 1.

290
00:32:02,431 --> 00:32:08,571
1. The wall made the attack stop. The Zulu weapon was the stabbing spear, and it needs contact.

291
00:32:09,091 --> 00:32:14,971
A four-foot wall of grain sacks turns a charge into a crowd, standing still in front of rifles.

292
00:32:15,511 --> 00:32:24,431
2. The line got shorter, three times. The biscuit boxes, then the crowl, then the redoubt. Each step back put fewer men closer together.

293
00:32:24,431 --> 00:32:28,191
The garrison never tried to hold more wall than it had rifles for.

294
00:32:28,691 --> 00:32:35,451
3. Firepower at close range. Each soldier carried a Martini Henry breech loader, and the store was full of ammunition.

295
00:32:36,051 --> 00:32:39,811
The few Zulus with guns on the hill had poor powder and poor aim.

296
00:32:40,371 --> 00:32:47,791
4. The attackers were exhausted and off their orders. This was the Zulu reserve from Isend-Lawana, after a 30-kilometer march.

297
00:32:47,791 --> 00:32:54,731
King Chechueyo had not wanted the frontier crossed. Most of the warriors had not eaten or rested since the day before.

298
00:32:55,050 --> 00:33:05,510
Go back to the wagons on the road to help Makar. Same men, same rifles, no wall. That was Eisendwana. Hold only the wall you can fill and have the next one ready behind it. If

299
00:33:05,510 --> 00:33:15,970
you have seen the 1964 film Zulu, this is the fight it was made from. The real walls were smaller. Subscribe for the next battle and tell us below which ground we should walk next.

